§ 50.63 Loss of all alternating current power.
(a) Each power reactor licensed under this part must be able to withstand and recover from a station blackout as defined in § 50.2. The station blackout duration for a particular plant shall be determined by considering the redundancy of the onsite emergency ac power sources, reliability of the onsite emergency and offsite power systems, expected frequency of station blackout, and the probable time needed to restore offsite power. The coping duration for a particular plant is the specified period of time that the plant must be able to withstand a station blackout and maintain core cooling and appropriate containment integrity. The coping duration shall be at least 4 hours or as determined by the NRC for each plant. Each applicant shall submit a station blackout evaluation that demonstrates the capability of the plant to cope with a station blackout for the specified duration. The evaluation must address the following:
(1) The capability of the onsite ac power system to cope with a station blackout, including the capacity and reliability of the onsite emergency ac power sources and the ability to restore offsite power within the coping duration;
(2) The capability of the plant to maintain core cooling and appropriate containment integrity during the station blackout coping duration, including the use of any alternate ac sources as defined in § 50.2;
(3) The capability of the plant to restore and maintain core cooling, containment integrity, and other vital functions following the restoration of offsite or onsite ac power sources after a station blackout event.

§ 50.63 Loss of all alternating current power.
(a) Each power reactor licensed to operate under this part and each applicant for a license to operate a power reactor under this part shall be able to withstand for a specified duration and recover from a station blackout as defined in § 50.2. The specified station blackout duration shall be based on the following factors:
(1) The redundancy of the onsite emergency ac power sources;
(2) The reliability of the onsite emergency and offsite ac power sources;
(3) The expected frequency of loss of offsite power;
(4) The probable time needed to restore offsite power;
(5) The probable time needed to repair and restore an onsite emergency ac power source to operable status;
(6) The capability and reliability of the onsite non-emergency ac power system (if any) to cope with a station blackout; and
(7) The capability of the station to cope with a station blackout, including the ability to maintain core cooling and containment integrity for the duration of the station blackout.
(b) The coping analysis must demonstrate that the capability and reliability of equipment and procedures are adequate to ensure that the core is cooled and containment integrity is maintained during the station blackout duration. The analysis must address the environmental conditions (e.g., temperature, humidity, radiation) that equipment must withstand during the station blackout and the capability of the equipment to perform its intended function for the duration of the station blackout.

