data.answer:
The regulatory requirements for minimizing the probability of rapidly propagating fracture in the reactor coolant pressure boundary (RCPB) during feedwater system pipe break transients are established primarily by General Design Criterion (GDC) 31, as referenced in both DSRS 15.2.8 and 10 CFR Part 50, Appendix A. GDC 31 mandates that the RCPB must be designed with sufficient margin to ensure nonbrittle behavior and to minimize the probability of rapidly propagating fracture under all relevant conditions, including operation, maintenance, testing, and postulated accident scenarios such as feedwater system pipe breaks. 

Specifically, the design must account for:
- Service temperatures and other environmental conditions experienced by the boundary material during all operational and accident conditions;
- The inherent properties of the boundary material, including uncertainties in determining these properties;
- The effects of irradiation on material properties;
- Residual, steady state, and transient stresses imposed on the boundary;
- The size of potential flaws in the boundary material.

Additionally, the design must include provisions for detecting flaws and periodic inspection, as well as means for detecting and, where practical, mitigating the effects of degradation. These requirements are particularly pertinent to feedwater system pipe breaks, as such events can cause rapid changes in reactor coolant system (RCS) temperature and pressure, potentially imposing undue stress on the RCPB. The severity of these transients and their impact on the RCPB must be assessed in the Safety Analysis Report (SAR) and reviewed in accordance with DSRS 15.2.8.

References: DSRS 15.2.8, Section II; 10 CFR Part 50, Appendix A, GDC 31.

